MView Day 26 (2026-10-08)
=========================

"Confocal" explained (user asked; no change)
--------------------------------------------
The box sets which kind of calibration a reference measurement makes:
ticked - it keeps the image width and zoom, and scales with them on
other images; unticked - a fixed px per µm. It is the dialog's default;
images with a scale of their own are not affected.

Three small changes (user, after testing M3b)
---------------------------------------------
1. "the measurement should not have a sub line, only the µm value":
   uMeasureLine's label is the µm only (no "(px)", no "scale: ..."
   line). Without a scale and while calibrating: px.
2. "the right click context menu entry 'create copy' for filter and
   rotate should also burn in the measurement":
   - uExtensions: burn-in for overlay layers (TOverlayLayer.CanBurnIn /
     BurnIn, TBurnInMap, BurnInPoint: image px -> copy px through the
     quick-view scale, the mirror and the turn as RotatedBitmap;
     OverlayCanBurnIn / OverlayBurnIn). MView: no layers, unchanged.
   - TMView.ApplyFiltersToCopy: after filters, mirror and turn, the
     layers draw into the copy; the name gets their part
     ('_56.32um'); CopyBurnsIn; CanApplyFilters also with a measurement
     only. Main form: the entry's caption says what goes in ("Apply
     filters and measurement to a copy", "... filters, rotation and
     measurement ...").
   - uMeasureLine: CanBurnIn (a finished line of this image, not a
     reference measurement), BurnIn (upright, sized for the copy);
     the drawing shared with the screen (PlaceLabel, DrawMeasure).
3. The pointer vanished when the left panel's "Scale" list was open and
   the mouse left it (screenshot): the hide-after-idle timer ran while
   the list was open (the right-click menu had a guard, the edition's
   popup didn't). uExtensions.NoteMenuOpen / OnMenuOpen; the main form
   treats it as its own menu (pointer shown, not hidden, the time in it
   not counted). Used by the scale list and the reference dialog.

Stacks, step 1: what a stack is (user: "we can move to the stacks")
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Of the three steps proposed (1 time vs depth and the cut-off frames;
2 the animation mark and the frame list on a left double-click; 3 the
playback on a right double-click), step 1, in uLifFile only:
- TLifImage: StackKind (none / depth / time), FrameCount (along t if
  there is time, else z), FakeZ, FrameTimes (s, from all time stamps:
  one per plane and channel, frame f's at f * stamps / frames),
  FrameInterval (the median step), Usable (cache).
- A z with step 0 (Begin = End) and running stamps = time at one plane;
  the panel line "Time: 12 points, every 2.593 s (filed as 12 z-planes
  at one depth)" instead of "Stack: 12 z-planes".
- UsableFrameCount: the last frame is cut off when its step is < 0.8 x
  the median (no times: nothing left out) and its bottom rows are 0 in all
  channels, H/16 more than the frame before. Checked against Series009
  (Python, from the user's file): last step 1.146 s of 2.593, 576 black
  rows in both channels, 0 in all others -> 11 usable.
- RenderRGBA(z = -1): the projection of the usable frames only (was:
  all z). RenderFrameRGBA: one frame. StackSummary: 'z 12 × 0.5 µm',
  't 11 × 2.6 s'.
- TestLif: TestStacks (a fake z made there: cut last frame left out, a
  full last step kept; the small depth stack), Series009 checks.
The container reader (uLifContainer) is unchanged: its projection now
leaves the cut frame out by itself.

Stacks, step 2: the mark, the note, the frames' list
----------------------------------------------------
(User: test run all passed, 0 failed - step 1 confirmed.)
- uContainers: TContainerEntry.Frames; RegisterContainerStacks (frames'
  list + note per reader); ListContainerEntries(virtual name) = a
  stack's frames; ContainerEntryNote.
- uLifContainer: ListLif marks stacks; FramesLif (usable frames, ids
  '<stack>|#t03', names 't 3    5.2 s' / 'z 3    1.00 µm'); NoteLif
  ('t 11 × 2.6 s (projection)'); DecodeLif renders a frame id;
  LifSplitFrame; LifIndexOfId takes a frame to its stack (scale);
  one cached TLifFile under a lock for the list and the notes.
- uContainerThumbs: a virtual "container" (a stack) works: entry names
  made with the container file; RGBAToBitmap exported.
- uSortPanel: TPanelEntry.Frames, the animation mark (cyan play
  triangle on the thumbnail's corner); a new list starts at its top.
- uMView: left double-click on a stack entry -> EnterFrames (first
  click shows it, as before, foot: "double-click: its frames"); back ->
  the .lif's list; a frame click -> ShowContainerFrame (decoded at once,
  shown like a crop with its virtual name as key: savable, measurable,
  yellow in the list); info line + ItemNote.
- TestLif: TestFrameEntries (LifSplitFrame, list / frames / decode /
  note through uContainers); build_test.bat: TestLif gets the core and
  utility paths.
Next: step 3, right double-click on the stack = play in the view.

The microscope's details on the large preview (user)
----------------------------------------------------
"can you overlay the lif data/microscopy details to the magnified
preview image?" (the picture left of the panel while the mouse is on an
entry of a .lif's list). uSortPanel: OnEntryDetails; ComposePreview
lays the lines on a dark band over the picture's lower part (the box
widened to 340 px for them; as many lines as fit, each cut with "...").
uMView.PanelEntryDetails: ReadImageMetadata(entry).Lines, one cached.
A frame shows its stack's details. (Before: the user's screenshot of
the info line was the stack shown, not a frame - fine as it is.)

MView: the edit-mode frame on a turned image (user)
---------------------------------------------------
"if an image is rotated, and then in edit mode a frame is drawn, it is
rotated with the image. that should not be the case."
Cause: the selection was a rectangle in the image's own pixels
(TImageRect), drawn through ImageToScreen - a turned rectangle.
Fix (both editions):
- uRenderer: TImageRect.Angle; SelectionAngle (the draw angle beyond
  the nearest quarter turn - review: quarter turns stay the old path -,
  its negative when mirrored: M R(a) = R(-a) M keeps upright rectangles
  upright); SelectionImageCorners (the corners turned back into the
  image's pixels); SelectionCorners uses them (CPU and GPU draw it).
- uMView.DragSelection: the anchor and the point in the image's pixels
  turned by the angle at the drag's start, kept inside the turned
  image's bounds; Angle 0 = exactly the old path.
- CropSelection: a turned selection is cut from RotatedBitmap(source,
  angle) (DecodeFits checked first, hourglass); shown turned by the
  view's angle less the selection's draw angle (0 unless turned since).
- RefreshHistogram: a turned selection -> its bounding box in the
  image's pixels. MakeIconFromImage: its middle.
Spec v1.3 §9.5 amended.

MView: Esc closes the left panel first (user)
---------------------------------------------
"if both side panels are open, esc should close the left panel first. it
is more reasonable to keep the right panel open all the time, but the
left one only casually." TMView.GoBack: filter panel, then sort panel,
then magnifier, edit mode, settings screen. Spec v1.3 (sort panel and
filter panel) amended.
User's order, replacing the above: "1 magnifier, 2 left panel, 3 edit
mode, 4 right panel, 5 edit mode, 6 program close" - read as: magnifier,
filter panel, edit mode, sort panel, settings screen (its own Esc ends
the program; the second "edit mode" taken as the settings screen, asked
in the reply). GoBack and the spec as such.

The left panel says where the µm come from (user)
-------------------------------------------------
"lif and stack, the measurement should be based on internal metadata. i
suppose it is, pse display this in the left panel". It was (ScaleFor:
the file's own scale first; a stack's frame maps to its stack); the row
said only "from the file". uCalibrationPanel: "lif metadata  0.1173
µm/px" inside a container (its extension), "file metadata  ..." for a
file's own; the hint "measured with the metadata: <value>".

Auto and the frame (user)
-------------------------
The user saw the black point move while drawing a frame: Auto for every
image was on and re-levels from the selection live (as designed, Phase
H). Explained; user: re-level "on mouse up". TMView: FSelDragging (set
by DragSelection, cleared by ClearSelection), the release in
HandleOverlayMouse sets FSelDragDone; the tick skips ApplyAuto while
dragging and applies it once when the drag is done. The histogram in
the panel still follows live. Spec v1.3 (Auto) amended.

The scale travels with the image; several measurements; label style (user)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. "do it please, important step!" - the in-memory part:
   - uExtensions: NoteDerivedImage / RegisterDerivedImageHandler
     (new name, parent, operation, parent size, parent px per new px,
     turn).
   - uMView: CropSelection ('crop', factor 1, the selection's turn),
     ApplyFiltersToCopy ('copy', factor 1, the view's turn),
     ResizeToShown ('resized', parent / new size) - before ShowScratch.
   - uImageScale: HandleDerived (the parent's ScaleFor, times the
     factors, quarter turns swap, oblique: mean; source 'Series009
     (crop)', a chain carries the parent's source on); ScaleFor asks it
     first (order 0); at most 200, the oldest go.
   - uCalibrationPanel: "from Series009 (crop)"; hint "measured with
     <value>".
   Saving the scale into the file: next (with the TIFF options).
2. "multiple measurements ... with the shift key": uMeasureLine keeps a
   list of finished lines; Shift at a line's first click keeps the
   earlier ones, without Shift the new line replaces them; all drawn,
   each with its label, in one picture; burned in all ('_3lines').
   Shift while following still = horizontal / vertical.
3. "link the text on the measurement to the transparency setting of the
   status bar": [View] OverlaySolid (renderer.OverlaySolid): solid black
   box, or no box and the text outlined; the burn-in the same.
Review (independent), taken: the derived scale is worked out lazily
from the parent (TDerivedRecord: parent, size, factors, turn,
operation; ComputeScale recursive, at most 20 deep) - a frozen value
would have ignored a calibration chosen later; a reference measurement
on a crop offers its inherited value; Shift held on from the first
click doesn't snap (pressed again it does); a cancelled reference
measurement leaves no line; the outline drawn in 8 directions (a
second ring for thick text) instead of a full square of offsets.

The saved file carries the scale (user: "please go!")
------------------------------------------------------
- uImageFileScale (new, source\utility, plain): MakeRgbTiff (baseline
  RGB, one strip; with a scale ImageJ's way: description
  "ImageJ=1.54f\nunit=micron\n", X/YResolution px per µm, unit none);
  AddPngPixelSize (pHYs after IHDR, CRC-32); ReadImageFileScale /
  ReadStreamScale (ImageJ TIFF unit micron/um/µm/\u00B5m/nm/mm; PNG
  pHYs in metres, below 100 µm per px).
- uImageSaver: SaveBitmapAs (.tif/.tiff -> TIFF, else PNG; with a scale
  the PNG gets pHYs); TImageSaveThread takes AUmX / AUmY; the result
  says "(with its scale, 0.1173 µm/px)".
- uExtensions: RegisterImageScaleQuery / QueryImageScale.
- uMView: SaveScale (the scale per pixel of the bitmap written: a quick
  view's is larger), passed by StartSave, SaveCurrentImage, SortScratch;
  SaveImageAs keeps .tif / .tiff. uMainForm: the dialog offers TIFF.
- uImageScale: QueryScale registered; the "File scale" metadata reader
  first (before the .lif guesses).
- TestCalibrations: TestFileScale; build_test.bat: + utility path.
Review (independent; the layout checked by porting the writer to Python
and reading it with Pillow): taken - SaveImageAs had lost its
"FileName := AFileName" (critical: every Save as wrote ".png"); a PNG's
scale is its own tEXt chunk "MView scale" (um_per_pixel=x,y; exact), the
pHYs alone (300 dpi from an image program) is no scale; µ spelled every
way ImageJ files use; pHYs and rationals clamped to 32 bits; Int64 for
TIFF offsets; the TIFF choice renames cell.jpg too and asks before
replacing a renamed file. Noted: the TIFF / scaled PNG are built in
memory (about twice the image) - fine below ~100 MP.

Intermission: Appose; the Lab in the workflow (user)
----------------------------------------------------
"do you consider using appose for communication with fiji?" - not now
(no attach to a running Fiji, no Pascal side, large environments);
later possibly an optional add-on for computing in the Lab. User:
"microscope -> (mview lab <-> fiji) -> inkscape/adobe/corel; once in
inkscape, no more image manipulation should be necessary" - leads to
measurements as vectors: an SVG figure export (image embedded as PNG,
lines, text labels, scale bar, layers, metadata); burning in stays.
EPS rejected (legacy). Written into the edition's spec as §9c and
decision 15. Order (user): stack playback, Windows codecs (both
editions), M3 (scale bar, SVG export), Fiji exchange.

Stacks, step 3: playing (user: "continue with the stack animation")
------------------------------------------------------------------
- uAnimation: TBitmapListAnimation (whole pictures + delays, owns
  them; plays forever; its cursor duplicates a frame).
- uContainers: TContainerEntry.Time (s since the first frame, -1 =
  unknown); uLifContainer fills it for a time series's frames.
- uMView: right double-click on a stack entry (or on a frame in its
  list) -> PlayStack: ListContainerEntries(stack), then one frame per
  tick of FPlayTimer (DecodeContainerEntry, RGBAToBitmap; the first
  frame sets the size and the memory budget: 512 MB, else shrunk by
  sqrt(budget / bytes), the original size kept); FinishStackLoad: the
  delays (median step -> speed so a frame lasts ~200 ms, never slower
  than real time; depth 150 ms), TDecodedImage with the animation,
  ShowScratch (key = the stack's name: its scale). Again = stop
  (ShowCurrent); again while reading = cancel. Moving on while reading
  cancels. BeginClose and Destroy cancel.
